1
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
The Snowman is a adventure, animation, family film directed by Dianne Jackson.

2
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
A young boy makes a snowman one Christmas Eve, which comes to life at midnight and
takes him on a magical adventure to the North Pole to meet Santa Claus.

3
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Older James / Narrator during original 1982 British television
broadcast (uncredited), played by Raymond Briggs.

4
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Older James (re-released/US version), played by David Bowie.

5
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Father Christmas - 20th Anniversary version, played by Mel Smith.

6
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:36,000
The Snowman is a 1982 British animated television film and symphonic poem
based on Raymond Briggs's 1978 picture book The Snowman.

7
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
It was directed by Dianne Jackson for Channel 4.

8
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:06,000
It was first shown on 26 December 1982, and was an immediate success.

9
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:21,000
It was nominated for Best Animated Short Film at the 55th
Academy Awards and won a BAFTA TV Award.

10
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
The story is told through pictures, action and music, scored by Howard Blake.

11
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
It has no dialogue, with the exception of the
central song, "Walking in the Air".

12
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,000
The orchestral score was performed by the Sinfonia of London and the song
was performed by Peter Auty, a St Paul's Cathedral choirboy.

13
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:21,000
The film ranked at number 71 on the 100 Greatest British Television Programmes, a list drawn up
by the British Film Institute in 2000, based on a vote by industry professionals.

14
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
It was voted number 4 in UKTV Gold's Greatest TV Christmas Moments.

15
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:51,000
It came third in Channel 4's poll of 100 Greatest Christmas Moments in 2004.

16
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Its broadcast, usually on Christmas Eve on Channel 4, has become
an annual festive event in the UK and Ireland.

17
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:21,000
A sequel, The Snowman and the Snowdog, was released in 2012.

18
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
The film explores themes of holiday, little boy, snowman, christmas, short film.

19
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:51,000
The tone is lighthearted.
